 - **Account vs member:** `users` is the login account (Laravel Auth); `entity_memberships` is the user's link to the entity with role.
 - **User↔entity cardinality:** `entity_memberships` is N:N by design (allows future multi-entity). In MVP, "one user per entity" rule is enforced in application; no global UK on `user_id`.
-- **Owner and circular FK:** `entities.owner_membership_id` points to the membership with `role = owner`. Since `entity_memberships.entity_id` references `entities`, the FK is circular — on bootstrap, create entity with `owner_membership_id` null, create owner membership, then fill the FK (or use deferrable constraint). Ownership transfer updates this FK and involved roles.
+- **Owner uniqueness (no circular FK):** there is no `entities.owner_membership_id`. The owner is the membership with `role = owner`. `entity_memberships.owner_seq` is a stored generated column (`CASE WHEN role = 'owner' THEN 1 ELSE NULL END`) with unique index `entity_memberships_one_owner` on `(entity_id, owner_seq)`. NULLs never collide in unique indexes (SQLite and MariaDB), so an entity can have one owner and any number of other members, while a second owner is rejected by the database. `owner_seq` is never written by the application. Ownership transfer must demote the current owner before promoting the new one, in one transaction.
+- **Entities:** soft deleted (`deleted_at`); bound in routes by `slug` (unique index `entities_slug_unique`). Memberships cascade on entity/user delete (FKs).
+- **Roles and authorization:** `App\Models\UserRole` (`owner`, `admin`, `employee`; `manageable()` = admin, employee). `EntityPolicy`: any authenticated user may create; members view; owner/admin update and delete; only the owner manages settings; non-members get nothing. `EntityMembershipPolicy`: members of the same entity view; owner/admin assign roles; delete is never allowed on the owner's membership, allowed for self-removal, otherwise owner/admin of that entity.
 - **Numeric priority:** `tickets.priority` is an integer — **higher value, more prioritized**. Named levels (normal/priority/80+) are configurable presets per entity mapping to values; sorting uses the number.
 - **Ticket write-once:** ticket is **single-use** and receives **one single call**. All fields are immutable after issuance, **except** `called_at` and `called_by_user_id`, filled at call time. No call history table.
 - **Ticket ↔ totem:** ticket knows only its **queue** (`queue_id`). `channel` records origin type; no FK to `totems`.
